<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vulca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vulca was an Etruscan sculptor of Veii, the only Etruscan artist named in Roman literature. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a> summoned him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to make the terracotta cult statue of Jupiter for the temple on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>. Modern scholars often attribute to his workshop the terracotta statues of the Portonaccio temple at Veii, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-of-veii">Apollo of Veii</a>.</p></div>
